+The encoding families analyzed here build on general results on quantum data encoding that predate QNLP. Schuld and Killoran \cite{schuld2019quantum} and Havl\'{\i}\v{c}ek et al.\ \cite{havlicek2019supervised} frame encoding as a quantum feature map and introduce the ZZ-type maps behind the structured feature-map family; LaRose and Coyle \cite{larose2020robust} compare angle, amplitude, and dense-angle encodings for classification and their robustness to noise; P\'erez-Salinas et al.\ \cite{perezsalinas2020data} introduce data re-uploading; M\"ott\"onen et al.\ \cite{mottonen2005transformation} and Shende et al.\ \cite{shende2006synthesis} give the generic state-preparation circuits whose depth underlies the cost of amplitude encoding; and Sim et al.\ \cite{sim2019expressibility} define the expressibility and entangling-capability measures behind the Sim14/Sim15 ans\"atze compared in the compositional studies. On the compositional side, DisCoCirc \cite{coecke2021mathematics} extends DisCoCat from sentences to text. Trainability results such as barren plateaus \cite{mcclean2018barren} and exponential kernel concentration \cite{thanasilp2024exponential} bound what any of these encodings can achieve at scale. None of these works addresses natural language specifically.
 
 Beyond QNLP itself, the quantum engineering literature has an established tradition of comprehensive subfield surveys, covering, for instance, quantum circuit compilation for NISQ devices \cite{kusyk2021survey}, quantum computing for finance \cite{egger2020quantum}, and entanglement routing in quantum networks \cite{abane2025entanglement}, the last of which likewise organizes its field through an explicit taxonomy of approaches. None of these addresses text encoding in QNLP, which is the gap the present survey fills within that tradition.
 
-This survey complements these works by treating encoding as the primary unit of analysis, yielding the first quantitative cross-study characterization of how text is encoded in QNLP and at what resource cost.
+This survey complements these works by treating encoding as the primary unit of analysis, yielding, to the best of our knowledge, the first systematic cross-study characterization of how text is encoded in QNLP and at what resource cost.
 
