 \subsection{Open Challenges and an Agenda for Future Work}
 \label{subsec:open_challenges}
 \label{subsec:future_agenda}
 
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item[C1)] \textbf{The encoding bottleneck.} Every non-syntactic encoding has to bridge the gap between the dimensionality of a useful text representation and the qubit budget of near-term hardware, and each pays for it in lost information, circuit depth, or classical preprocessing. In the corpus, embeddings of 768 dimensions are reduced to ten, four, or two features before the circuit \cite{ahmad_hybrid_2023,kumar_hybrid_2026,altintas_beyond_2025}, and state-preparation cost is routinely left out of the reported qubit budget. The proposed mitigations \cite{zhang_entangled_2024,tiblias_efficient_2026,silver_lexiql_2024} change which cost is paid, and no study measures what the reduction removes. Studies should report the retained dimensionality and the preparation depth alongside the qubit count, and the result of a classical model on the same reduced input.
+\item[C1)] \textbf{The encoding bottleneck.} Every non-syntactic encoding has to bridge the gap between the dimensionality of a useful text representation and the qubit budget of near-term hardware, and each pays for it in lost information, circuit depth, or classical preprocessing. In the corpus, embeddings of 768 dimensions are reduced to ten, four, or two features before the circuit \cite{ahmad_hybrid_2023,kumar_hybrid_2026,altintas_beyond_2025}, and state-preparation cost is routinely left out of the reported qubit budget. Table~\ref{tab:resource_model} makes the trade explicit: each family moves the cost of loading $d$ features into a different column, from register width (angle, structured maps) to preparation depth (amplitude), circuit depth (re-uploading), post-selection (DisCoCat), classical preprocessing (hybrid embedding), or measurement (Hamiltonian). The proposed mitigations \cite{zhang_entangled_2024,tiblias_efficient_2026,silver_lexiql_2024} change which cost is paid, and no study measures what the reduction removes. The corpus does not allow a systematic plot of source dimensionality against qubit count, because the dimensionality entering the circuit is reported inconsistently; this is itself part of the reporting gap the challenge describes. Studies should report the retained dimensionality and the preparation depth alongside the qubit count, and the result of a classical model on the same reduced input.
 
 \item[C2)] \textbf{Incommensurable evaluation.} Dataset, classical comparison, and protocol are chosen study by study, and 15 studies report no classical baseline. Even the one structurally motivated pairing, amplitude encoding on similarity tasks, is presented by its own study as a reproduction of classical similarity rather than an improvement over it \cite{cavar_word_2025,cavar_old_2026}. A small set of shared, encoding-agnostic benchmarks with fixed classical baselines would let a future meta-analysis attribute a performance gap to the encoding and not to the baseline a group selected. Controlled comparisons that hold model family, dataset, and circuit depth fixed and vary only the encoding or ansatz would separate the encoding from the architecture it is usually bound to.
 
-\item[C3)] \textbf{The gap between simulation and hardware.} Real-device validation peaked in 2024 and then fell sharply; the only hardware-validated studies of 2025 and 2026 are just two studies \cite{baik_neural_2025,liu_experimentally_2026}. Noiseless simulation says nothing about decoherence, gate error, or readout noise, the properties that decide near-term viability. Accuracy under an explicit, calibrated noise model should therefore be the minimum for publication, especially for angle and amplitude encoding and for recurrent architectures, which have no noisy-simulation or hardware study in the corpus.
+\item[C3)] \textbf{The gap between simulation and hardware.} Real-device validation peaked in 2024 and then fell sharply; the only hardware-validated studies of 2025 and 2026 are just two studies \cite{baik_neural_2025,liu_experimentally_2026}. Noiseless simulation says nothing about decoherence, gate error, or readout noise, the properties that decide near-term viability. Studies that make claims about near-term execution should therefore report accuracy under an explicit, calibrated noise model at minimum, together with gate counts, depth, and connectivity assumptions, while studies that report only ideal simulation should limit their claims to idealized conditions. This applies especially to angle and amplitude encoding, which engage noise least often, and for recurrent architectures, which have no noisy-simulation or hardware study in the corpus.
 
-\item[C4)] \textbf{Absent scaling evidence.} Only 23 studies reports test with more than one register width, and systematic sweeps such as the comparisons from 1 to 16 qubits in \cite{zhang_entangled_2024,kim_quantum_2025} are rare. The resource arguments made for each encoding are asymptotic, while the evidence is a single point. A register-width sweep should accompany any claim about scaling.
+\item[C4)] \textbf{Absent scaling evidence.} Only 23 studies test more than one register width, and systematic sweeps such as the comparisons from 1 to 16 qubits in \cite{zhang_entangled_2024,kim_quantum_2025} are rare. The resource arguments made for each encoding are asymptotic, while the evidence is a single point. A register-width sweep should accompany any claim about scaling.
 
-\item[C5)] \textbf{Reproducibility and trivial benchmarks.} Most studies release no code, and many do not name their simulation framework. Only 7 datasets recur in four or more papers: generic sentiment benchmarks with no QNLP-specific signal (IMDb, 12 papers; Amazon reviews, 8; Yelp, 7) and the synthetic MC and RP corpora of about 100 sentences built into \textsc{lambeq} (12 and 9 papers), on which near-perfect accuracies are reported \cite{lorenz_qnlp_2023,bouakba_ensemble_2023,liu_research_2024}. Releasing code, naming the framework and backend, and replacing toy corpora with the shared benchmarks of C2 would make independent re-execution possible.
+\item[C5)] \textbf{Reproducibility and trivial benchmarks.} Most studies release no code, and many do not name their simulation framework. Only 7 datasets recur in four or more papers: generic sentiment benchmarks with no QNLP-specific signal (IMDb, 12 papers; Amazon reviews, 8; Yelp, 7; SST-2, 5), the synthetic MC and RP corpora of about 100 sentences built into \textsc{lambeq} (12 and 9 papers), on which near-perfect accuracies are reported \cite{lorenz_qnlp_2023,bouakba_ensemble_2023,liu_research_2024}, and the restaurant-domain sentences introduced with SimpleSA (5 papers). Releasing code, naming the framework and backend, and replacing toy corpora with the shared benchmarks of C2 would make independent re-execution possible.
 \end{enumerate}
 
 One further direction goes beyond repairing current practice. The single documented fit between task and encoding yields efficiency and not a better result, because the encoded state reproduces the classical geometry it loads. Future work should look deliberately for tasks in which the mathematical structure of an encoding performs the core computation and that computation is classically expensive. Relational tasks such as question answering, where syntactic encoding has a theoretical fit that the corpus is too thin to evaluate, are one candidate. A targeted study of this kind, as opposed to another general-purpose classifier, could show whether a structural fit ever produces a gain against a fixed classical baseline.
 
+\section{Threats to Validity}
+\label{sec:threats}
+
+\textit{Search coverage.} Only Scopus and IEEE Xplore were searched. ACM Digital Library, Web of Science, and preprint servers such as arXiv were not queried directly, and 30 of the 125 studies (24\%) entered only through snowballing, which indicates that the database queries alone had limited recall. Snowballing from all 33 surveys, forward and backward over two rounds, mitigates this, but a study neither cited by nor citing any survey remains invisible to the procedure (Section~\ref{subsec:snowball}).
+\textit{Selection and protocol deviations.} Some included studies sit at the boundary of the criteria and are reported here as explicit deviations. Quanthoven \cite{miranda_quantum_2022} is a book chapter on music, retained for its grammar-to-circuit pipeline; it entered through citation search, which applies no document-type filter. Three studies on source-code vulnerability detection \cite{zhou_new_2022,song_recurrent_2024,akter_automated_2023}, one on SQL cardinality estimation \cite{uotila_sql2circuits_2025}, and one on steganalysis \cite{roshan_ahmed_deepket-_2024} were admitted under the IC1 clarification for tokenized, NLP-embedded sequences. The quantum-network study \cite{liu_experimentally_2026} was admitted because it trains its federated model on a sentiment-classification task. Three two-page papers \cite{ahmad_hybrid_2023,hamilton_characterizing_2023,aktar_quantum_2025} were retained because they specify encoding, circuit, and evaluation (EC3). Two research threads have related papers that EC4 could have merged: Cavar et al.\ \cite{cavar_semantic_2024,cavar_word_2025,cavar_old_2026} and Wazni et al.\ \cite{wazni_quantum_2022,wazni_large_2024}; each paper was kept because it reports a distinct experimental setting rather than an extended version of the same one. Removing all thirteen borderline studies (112 remain) leaves the key contrasts unchanged: angle and amplitude encoding engage noise or hardware in 12 of 72 studies (16.7\%) against 15 of 40 for the rest (37.5\%; Fisher $p = 0.020$), and hardware-touched studies fall from 10 of 48 before 2025 to 1 of 64 in 2025--2026 (Fisher $p < 0.001$).
+
+\textit{Classification.} The single-label rule for encoding (Section~\ref{subsec:analysis_framework}) discards secondary encodings in about one study in nine, and the boundaries between angle encoding, data re-uploading, and hybrid embedding rest on decision rules rather than on disjoint mechanisms. Residual misclassification would mainly move studies between angle encoding and the two emerging families that border it.
+
+\textit{Author clustering.} Several findings rest on few studies from few groups. Ruskanda and co-authors contribute six studies \cite{ruskanda_quantum_2022,ruskanda_simple_2023,ruskanda_quantum-enhanced_2023,ruskanda_enhancing_2024,ruskanda_evaluating_2025,abiwardani_generalized_2025}, including two of the three studies behind the noun-freezing result (Section~\ref{subsec:syntactic_across_ansatze}) and one of the two comparisons behind the weak showing of structured feature maps (Section~\ref{subsec:structured_across_constructions}). Cavar and co-authors contribute three of the six amplitude-encoded similarity studies (Section~\ref{sec:task_fit}). These findings are therefore stated as observations about the corpus, not as replicated results.
+
+\textit{Generalization from corpus to field.} Every count is a complete enumeration of the corpus. Claims about the field treat the corpus as a sample of its output; the key contrasts are accompanied by Fisher exact tests and confidence intervals, and the remaining cross-tabulations are descriptive. The 2026 counts are partial (Section~\ref{subsec:search}).
+
 \section{Conclusion}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 
 This survey isolated text encoding as the unit of analysis in QNLP and classified 125 studies published between 2021 and 2026 into seven encoding families. Its central result is that the literature cannot yet answer the question it implicitly poses, which encoding works better for language. What the corpus does support is structural: every non-syntactic family resolves the encoding bottleneck by choosing which cost to pay, discarded information, circuit depth, or classical preprocessing.
 
+The five research questions receive the following answers.
+\begin{itemize}
+\item[RQ1)] Text encodings in QNLP can be organized by the point at which text enters the circuit into seven families: four major (angle, 51 studies; syntactic/DisCoCat, 29; amplitude, 28; structured feature maps, 9) and three emerging (data re-uploading, hybrid embedding, Hamiltonian/observable encoding; 8 studies together).
+\item[RQ2)] No. Encoding cannot be compared independently of the architecture hosting it: syntactic encoding coincides with the compositional model family, structured maps sit mostly in kernels, and only angle encoding spreads across model families. Within each family, the role of the encoding, and hence what its results mean, changes with the host.
+\item[RQ3)] The field has moved from a compositional monoculture (all studies in 2021) to a mix dominated by angle and amplitude encoding in VQCs and hybrid models (71\% of 2025 studies; 2026 is a partial year). This shift is consistent with, but does not demonstrate, a preference for the lowest-effort designs over the most linguistically motivated ones.
+\item[RQ4)] Mostly no. The encoding mix within classification tasks reproduces the corpus-wide mix. The one principled fit, amplitude encoding for similarity, follows from the construction and yields efficiency, not superiority.
+\item[RQ5)] Weakly. 76.8\% of studies use noiseless simulation only, hardware validation fell to 2.9\% of studies in 2025--2026, 20\% release code, and 122 of 141 datasets are used by a single paper, so results cannot be compared across studies.
+\end{itemize}
+
 The corpus also shows what is missing. No study holds model family, dataset, and circuit depth fixed while varying only the encoding, and few report the retained dimensionality, the state-preparation cost, or a classical baseline on the same reduced input. These are the measurements that would turn the structural reading of this survey into an empirical one, and the agenda of Section~\ref{sec:trends_challenges} is built around them.
 
 Until such studies exist, encoding should be reported and justified as a design choice in its own right, with the same care given to the ansatz, the optimizer, and the classical baseline.
